[Term]
id: AISM:0004235
name: ectodermal gland {AISM:0000171="Snodgrass RE (1993) Principles of Insect Morphology. Cornell University Press, 768 pp.\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/10.7591/j.ctv1nhm1j\n\n\"cells or groups of cells with highly specialized secretory functions, producing a great variety of substances which are discharged at the exterior or into invaginations of the body wall\""}
def: "The region of cuticle that is continuous with glandular epithelial cells." [] {creation_date="2021-08-17", x_1.1:contributor="imiko https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2938-9075"}
is_a: AISM:0000174 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! insect region of cuticle
relationship: RO:0002150 CL:0000150 {x_1.1:contributor="imiko https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2938-9075"} ! continuous with glandular epithelial cell

[Term]
id: AISM:0004236
name: unicellular ectodermal gland {AISM:0000171="Snodgrass RE (1993) Principles of Insect Morphology. Cornell University Press, 768 pp.\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/10.7591/j.ctv1nhm1j\n\n\"one-celled gland usually larger than the cells surrounding it; discharges its products directly through the covering cuticula. [...] In many glands a minute cuticular ductule extends from the exterior into the body of each cell, allowing the secretion to pass out through an extremely thin layer of cuticula.\""}
def: "The ectodermal gland that is composed of one cell" [] {creation_date="2021-08-16", x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"}
is_a: AISM:0004235 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! ectodermal gland
relationship: BFO:0000051 CL:0000000 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! has part cell

[Term]
id: AISM:0004237
name: cuticular lobe {term:modified="2021-12-08", AISM:0000171="Nichols, S. W. (1989) Torre-Bueno glossary of entomology. New York Entomological Society. 840 pp.\n\nhttps://ipmhouse.tamu.edu/files/2021/03/The-Torre-Bueno-Glossary-of-Entomology.pdf\n\n\"a rounded projection or protuberance\""}
def: "The cuticular evagination that is largely membranous." [] {creation_date="2021-06-14", x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"}
is_a: AISM:0000128 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883", creation_date="2021-06-14"} ! multicellular cuticular evagination
relationship: BFO:0000051 AISM:0000004 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883", creation_date="2021-06-14"} ! has part conjunctiva

[Term]
id: AISM:0004238
name: multicellular ectodermal gland {AISM:0000171="Snodgrass RE (1993) Principles of Insect Morphology. Cornell University Press, 768 pp.\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/10.7591/j.ctv1nhm1j\n\n\"many-celled glands may be a group of cells situated at the surface of the body, but most of them are invaginations of the body wall.\""}
def: "The ectodermal gland that is composed of multiple cells." [] {creation_date="2021-08-16", x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"}
is_a: AISM:0004235 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! ectodermal gland

[Term]
id: AISM:0004239
name: occiput {AISM:0000171="Snodgrass RE (1993) Principles of Insect Morphology. Cornell University Press, 768 pp.\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/10.7591/j.ctv1nhm1j\n\n\"dorsal part of the posterior surface of the head between the occipital and the postoccipital sutures\""}
def: "The region of cuticle on the posterodorsal region of the head capsule that is posterior to the occipital ridge and anterior to the postoccipital ridge." [] {creation_date="2021-08-16", x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"}
is_a: AISM:0000174 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! insect region of cuticle
relationship: BSPO:0000096 AISM:0004021 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! anterior to postoccipital ridge
relationship: BSPO:0000099 AISM:0004048 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! posterior to occipital ridge

[Term]
id: AISM:0004240
name: epicranial suture {AISM:0000171="Beutel RG, Friedrich F, Yang X-K, Ge S-Q (2014) Insect Morphology and Phylogeny Insect morphology and phylogeny: a textbook for students of entomology. De Gruyter.\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1515/9783110264043\n\n\"Y-shaped ecdysial sutures dividing vertex and frons, composed of\ncoronal suture and paired frontal sutures.\""}
def: "The cuticular suture composed of both the coronal suture and paired frontal sutures." [] {creation_date="2021-08-16", term:modified="2021-12-10", x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"}
is_a: AISM:0004028 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! cuticular suture
relationship: BFO:0000051 AISM:0004029 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! has part frontal suture
relationship: BFO:0000051 AISM:0004030 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! has part coronal suture

[Term]
id: AISM:0004241
name: temple {AISM:0000171="Nichols, S. W. (1989) Torre-Bueno glossary of entomology. New York Entomological Society. 840 pp.\n\nhttps://ipmhouse.tamu.edu/files/2021/03/The-Torre-Bueno-Glossary-of-Entomology.pdf\n\n\"the part of the head above and behind the compound eyes\""}
def: "The region of cuticle that is behind the compound eye." [] {creation_date="2021-08-16", x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"}
is_a: AISM:0000174 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! insect region of cuticle
relationship: BSPO:0000099 AISM:0004052 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! posterior to insect compound eye

[Term]
id: AISM:0004242
name: insect wing region {AISM:0000171="Nichols, S. W. (1989) Torre-Bueno glossary of entomology. New York Entomological Society. 840 pp.\n\nhttps://ipmhouse.tamu.edu/files/2021/03/The-Torre-Bueno-Glossary-of-Entomology.pdf\n\n\"the principal areas of the wings differentiated in the wing-flexing insects (Neoptera), and often separated by distinct lines of folding, including the wing base, remigium, clavus, and jugum.\""}
def: "The region of cuticle of the insect wing that has wing veins, folds, and conjunctiva." [] {creation_date="2021-09-07", x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"}
is_a: AISM:0000174 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! insect region of cuticle
relationship: BFO:0000051 AISM:0004243 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! has part insect wing vein
relationship: BFO:0000051 AISM:0004244 {x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"} ! has part insect wing fold

[Term]
id: AISM:0004243
name: insect wing vein {AISM:0000171="Nichols, S. W. (1989) Torre-Bueno glossary of entomology. New York Entomological Society. 840 pp.\n\nhttps://ipmhouse.tamu.edu/files/2021/03/The-Torre-Bueno-Glossary-of-Entomology.pdf\n\n\"sclerotized, tubular structure supporting the membrane of the wings\""}
def: "The region of cuticle of the insect wing that is less flexible than the wing conjunctiva." [] {creation_date="2021-09-07", x_1.1:contributor="jgiron https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0851-6883"}
